        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/e04cb325/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator-command/requirements.md",
        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-01-30\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line utility that validates YAML file syntax, providing developers with a quick way to check whether their YAML configuration files are syntactically correct before deployment or further processing. The command accepts a file path argument and returns clear pass/fail feedback with actionable error information.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files need a quick, local method to validate file syntax without relying on external tools or services. Invalid YAML files can cause application failures, deployment issues, or configuration errors that are difficult to debug without clear error messages indicating the exact location of syntax problems.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces time spent debugging malformed YAML files\n- Catches syntax errors early in the development workflow\n- Provides consistent validation across the development team\n- Integrates seamlessly into existing npm/bun-based workflows\n- Zero additional dependency overhead maintains lean project footprint\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Valid YAML files correctly identified | 100% |\n| Invalid YAML files correctly rejected | 100% |\n| Error messages include line number | 100% of syntax errors |\n| Command execution time | < 1 second for typical config files |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Need |\n|-----------|-------------|--------------|\n| Developer | Team members creating/editing YAML files | Quick syntax validation during development |\n| CI/CD Pipeline | Automated build processes | Programmatic validation with exit codes |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Development Team | Reliable, fast YAML validation without new dependencies |\n| Project Maintainers | Minimal maintenance burden, no new package dependencies |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Syntax validation of YAML files (structural correctness)\n- Support for .yaml file extension\n- Support for .yml file extension\n- File path argument input method\n- Human-readable pass/fail output messages\n- Line number reporting for syntax errors\n- Proper exit codes (0 for valid, non-zero for invalid)\n- npm script integration in package.json\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (validating against a specific structure)\n- Semantic validation (validating values/content meaning)\n- Multiple file validation (glob patterns, directories)\n- Stdin input method\n- JSON output format\n- Auto-fix capabilities\n- Watch mode / file monitoring\n- Remote file validation (URLs)\n- YAML to JSON conversion\n- Pretty-printing or formatting\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Bun runtime is available in the execution environment\n- Users have basic familiarity with command-line tools\n- YAML files are stored on the local filesystem\n- Existing project dependencies or built-in capabilities provide YAML parsing\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: File Path Input\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall accept a single file path as a command-line argument |\n| Rationale | Developers need to specify which file to validate |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Command invoked as `bun run validate <filepath>` correctly receives the file path |\n\n### REQ-002: YAML Extension Support\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall accept files with .yaml or .yml extensions |\n| Rationale | Both extensions are commonly used for YAML files in the industry |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Files with .yaml extension are processed; Files with .yml extension are processed |\n\n### REQ-003: Syntax Validation\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall validate whether the file content is syntactically valid YAML |\n| Rationale | Core purpose of the feature is to detect YAML syntax errors |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Valid YAML content passes validation; Invalid YAML content fails validation |\n\n### REQ-004: Success Output\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall display a human-readable success message when validation passes |\n| Rationale | Users need clear confirmation that their file is valid |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Success message is displayed for valid YAML files |\n\n### REQ-005: Error Output with Line Numbers\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall display human-readable error messages including line numbers when validation fails |\n| Rationale | Line numbers help developers quickly locate and fix syntax errors |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Error message includes line number where syntax error was detected; Error message is human-readable |\n\n### REQ-006: Exit Code - Success\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| Requirement | The command shall exit with code 0 when the YAML file is valid |\n| Rationale | Standard Unix convention for success enables CI/CD integration |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Exit code is 0 for valid YAML files |\n\n### REQ-007: Exit Code - Failure\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| Requirement | The command shall exit with a non-zero code when the YAML file is invalid |\n| Rationale | Standard Unix convention for failure enables CI/CD integration |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Exit code is non-zero for invalid YAML files |\n\n### REQ-008: npm Script Registration\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall be registered as an npm script named \"validate\" in package.json |\n| Rationale | Consistent with project conventions for running commands via npm/bun |\n| Acceptance Criteria | `bun run validate <filepath>` invokes the validation command |\n\n### REQ-009: Missing File Handling\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall display an error message and exit with non-zero code when the specified file does not exist |\n| Rationale | Users need clear feedback when file path is incorrect |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Appropriate error message displayed for non-existent files; Exit code is non-zero |\n\n### REQ-010: Missing Argument Handling\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Should Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall display usage instructions when invoked without a file path argument |\n| Rationale | Helps users understand correct command usage |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Usage message displayed when no argument provided |\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Requirement | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation time for files under 1MB | < 1 second |\n| Command startup time | < 500ms |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Local file access only | Command shall only read files from the local filesystem |\n| Read-only operation | Command shall not modify the input file |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Clear error messages | Error output shall be understandable without YAML expertise |\n| Consistent output format | Pass/fail messages shall follow consistent formatting |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| No new dependencies | Implementation shall use only existing project dependencies or built-in capabilities |\n| YAML 1.1/1.2 compatibility | Validation shall handle standard YAML syntax |\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate YAML File\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| As a | Developer |\n| I want to | run a validation command on my YAML file |\n| So that | I can confirm the file has correct syntax before using it |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)**:\n- GIVEN I have a YAML file at path `config.yaml`\n- WHEN I run `bun run validate config.yaml`\n- THEN I see a pass or fail message indicating the validation result\n\n### STORY-002: Locate Syntax Errors\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| As a | Developer |\n| I want to | see the line number where a syntax error occurs |\n| So that | I can quickly navigate to and fix the problem |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)**:\n- GIVEN I have a YAML file with a syntax error on line 15\n- WHEN I run the validate command on that file\n- THEN I see an error message that includes \"line 15\" or similar line reference\n\n### STORY-003: CI/CD Integration\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| As a | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| I want to | receive appropriate exit codes from validation |\n| So that | I can fail the build when YAML files are invalid |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)**:\n- GIVEN the validation command is run in a CI environment\n- WHEN the YAML file is valid\n- THEN the exit code is 0\n- AND WHEN the YAML file is invalid\n- THEN the exit code is non-zero\n\n### STORY-004: Validate YML Extension\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| As a | Developer |\n| I want to | validate files with .yml extension |\n| So that | I can use the validator regardless of which extension convention I follow |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)**:\n- GIVEN I have a YAML file named `config.yml`\n- WHEN I run `bun run validate config.yml`\n- THEN the file is validated the same as a .yaml file\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | Only one file shall be validated per command invocation | Keeps the command simple and predictable |\n| BR-002 | Validation is syntax-only, not schema-based | Scope limitation to maintain simplicity |\n| BR-003 | Exit code 0 indicates valid, non-zero indicates invalid or error | Unix convention for command-line tools |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| Constraint | No new npm dependencies may be added |\n| Constraint | Must work with Bun runtime |\n| Dependency | Requires existing YAML parsing capability (built-in or existing dependency) |\n| Dependency | Requires filesystem access for reading files |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Resolution | Source |\n|----------|------------|--------|\n| Input method | File path argument only | User clarification |\n| Validation level | Syntax validation only | User clarification |\n| Output format | Human-readable pass/fail with line numbers | User clarification |\n| Exit codes | 0 = valid, non-zero = invalid | User clarification |\n| File extensions | Both .yaml and .yml supported | User clarification |\n| Multiple files | Single file only, no glob support | User clarification |\n| Script name | \"validate\" in package.json | User clarification |\n| Dependencies | No new dependencies allowed | User clarification |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None | No PRDs found in .rp1/work/prds/ |\n| KB context | Best-effort without KB | index.md and concept_map.md not found |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| Error exit code value | Non-zero (1) | Unix convention default |\n| Empty file handling | Treat as valid (empty YAML is valid) | YAML specification |\n| Large file handling | No explicit limit | Conservative default - let runtime handle |\n| Character encoding | UTF-8 assumed | Industry standard default |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
    },
