        "content": "# Hypothesis Document: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Created**: 2026-01-30T09:47:00Z | **Status**: VALIDATED\n\n## Hypotheses\n### HYP-001: Custom YAML Syntax Validator Feasibility\n**Risk Level**: HIGH\n**Status**: REJECTED\n**Statement**: A custom-implemented YAML syntax validator can adequately detect common syntax errors without using external parsing libraries\n**Context**: This hypothesis is critical for the yaml-validator-command feature as it determines whether we can build a lightweight validator or need to rely on external dependencies.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: Custom validator correctly identifies syntax errors in test cases covering: indentation errors, unclosed quotes, invalid key-value syntax, empty files, and list formatting errors\n- REJECT if: Custom validator fails to detect obvious syntax errors that external parsers catch, or produces false positives on valid YAML\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n## Validation Findings\n\n### HYP-001 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-01-30T09:48:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT + EXTERNAL_RESEARCH\n**Result**: REJECTED\n\n**Evidence**:\n\nA comprehensive code experiment was conducted comparing a custom YAML syntax validator against the js-yaml library (a well-established YAML parser). The experiment tested 20 cases covering:\n\n- Indentation errors (tabs, inconsistent spacing)\n- Unclosed quotes (single and double)\n- Invalid key-value syntax\n- Empty file handling\n- List formatting\n- Edge cases (URLs, multiline strings, anchors, flow sequences)\n\n**Experiment Results:**\n- Total Test Cases: 20\n- True Positives (both invalid): 3\n- True Negatives (both valid): 15\n- False Positives (false alarm): 0\n- **False Negatives (missed errors): 2**\n\n**Critical Failures - Missed Syntax Errors:**\n\n1. **Inconsistent Indentation Detection**\n   - Test case: `parent:\\n  child1: a\\n   child2: b` (2 spaces then 3 spaces)\n   - js-yaml correctly reports: \"bad indentation of a mapping entry\"\n   - Custom validator: PASSED (incorrectly marked as valid)\n\n2. **Invalid Mapping Structure**\n   - Test case: `: no key` (colon without a key)\n   - js-yaml correctly reports: \"incomplete explicit mapping pair; a key node is missed\"\n   - Custom validator: PASSED (incorrectly marked as valid)\n\n**Metrics:**\n- Accuracy: 90.0%\n- Precision: 100.0%\n- **Recall: 60.0%** (unacceptable for a validator)\n- F1 Score: 75.0%\n\n**External Research Findings:**\n\nResearch confirms YAML parsing is inherently complex:\n\n1. **Specification Complexity**: The YAML spec is 23,449 words vs JSON's 1,969 words, with 10 chapters numbered four levels deep.\n\n2. **Version Inconsistencies**: YAML 1.2 differs substantially from 1.1. The same document can parse differently under different versions (e.g., `22:22` becomes `1342` in YAML 1.1 but stays as string in 1.2).\n\n3. **Implicit Type Coercion**: Values like `no`, `on`, `off` may become booleans. Version strings like `10.23` may become floats.\n\n4. **Context-Dependent Parsing**: Even parsing strings is context-dependent in YAML. Plain scalars inside flow sequences cannot contain certain characters.\n\n5. **Multi-line String Complexity**: There are 63 different multi-line string syntaxes in YAML.\n\n6. **Parser Inconsistencies**: Different parsers implement incompatible subsets or hybrids of the specification. Even mature parsers fail edge cases in the YAML test suite.\n\n**Sources**:\n- Experiment code: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/experiment-final.ts`\n- js-yaml library comparison (v4.1.1)\n- https://ruudvanasseldonk.com/2023/01/11/the-yaml-document-from-hell\n- https://www.arp242.net/yaml-config.html\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YAML\n- https://medium.com/@VincentDB/i-wrote-a-yaml-parser-from-scratch-in-zig-heres-what-i-learned-ee41ca477963\n\n**Implications for Design**:\n\n1. **Do NOT implement a custom YAML syntax validator** - The experiment demonstrates that even a reasonably sophisticated custom validator will miss critical syntax errors that users expect to be caught.\n\n2. **Use an external YAML parsing library** - Libraries like js-yaml, yaml (npm), or PyYAML have years of development and handle edge cases properly.\n\n3. **Consider the validation scope**:\n   - If the goal is \"basic sanity checks,\" a custom validator provides ~90% accuracy but WILL miss real errors\n   - If the goal is \"reliable syntax validation,\" an external parser is required\n\n4. **Potential compromise**: Use an external parser for validation, but implement custom checks for project-specific style rules (e.g., requiring spaces after colons, consistent indentation width) as WARNINGS, not errors.\n\n5. **Dependency trade-off**: js-yaml is ~48KB minified. This is a reasonable size for reliable YAML validation vs the risk of shipping a broken validator.\n\n## Summary\n| Hypothesis | Risk | Result | Implication |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------|\n| HYP-001 | HIGH | REJECTED | Custom YAML validator cannot adequately detect common syntax errors - external parsing library required |\n"
