        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-01-30\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line utility that validates YAML files for syntax correctness, providing clear feedback on validation results. This feature enables users to verify YAML file integrity without introducing additional external dependencies to the project.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers working with YAML configuration files need a straightforward way to validate file syntax before using them in their workflows. Currently, there is no built-in validation capability in the project, requiring users to rely on external tools or discover errors only at runtime.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces time spent debugging malformed YAML files\n- Prevents downstream errors caused by invalid configuration\n- Maintains project simplicity by avoiding new dependency additions\n- Provides immediate feedback during development workflows\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Validation accuracy | 100% detection of syntax errors |\n| Command execution time | Less than 1 second for typical files |\n| User adoption | Command is used in development workflow |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Needs |\n|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| Developer | Project contributor working with YAML configs | Quick validation during development |\n| CI/CD Pipeline | Automated build process | Programmatic validation with exit codes |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainers | Keep dependency footprint minimal |\n| Developers | Fast, reliable validation feedback |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Validation of YAML file syntax correctness\n- Command-line interface for validation\n- Clear error messages indicating validation problems\n- Support for single file validation\n- Exit codes for programmatic use (success/failure)\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (validating against a specific YAML schema)\n- YAML file editing or auto-correction\n- Batch validation of multiple files in one command\n- YAML to JSON conversion\n- Adding new npm dependencies\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n| ID | Assumption |\n|----|------------|\n| A1 | Bun runtime provides sufficient YAML parsing capabilities or JavaScript native methods can be used |\n| A2 | Users will provide file paths as command arguments |\n| A3 | Files to be validated are accessible on the local filesystem |\n| A4 | YAML files use standard YAML 1.1 or 1.2 specification |\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: Validate YAML File Syntax\n\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer, CI/CD Pipeline |\n| Requirement | The system shall accept a file path argument and validate that the file contains syntactically correct YAML |\n| Rationale | Core functionality needed to detect malformed YAML before use |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Given a valid YAML file, when the validator is run, then it reports success. Given an invalid YAML file, when the validator is run, then it reports the syntax error. |\n\n### REQ-002: Provide Clear Error Messages\n\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | When validation fails, the system shall display an error message indicating the nature and location of the syntax error |\n| Rationale | Developers need actionable feedback to fix problems |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Given an invalid YAML file, when validation fails, then the error message includes line number (if available) and error description |\n\n### REQ-003: Return Appropriate Exit Codes\n\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| Requirement | The command shall return exit code 0 on successful validation and a non-zero exit code on failure |\n| Rationale | Enables integration with automated pipelines and scripts |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Given successful validation, exit code is 0. Given failed validation, exit code is non-zero. |\n\n### REQ-004: Handle Missing or Inaccessible Files\n\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer, CI/CD Pipeline |\n| Requirement | The system shall display a clear error message when the specified file does not exist or cannot be read |\n| Rationale | Users need to understand why validation cannot proceed |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Given a non-existent file path, when the validator is run, then an appropriate error message is displayed and exit code is non-zero |\n\n### REQ-005: No New Dependencies\n\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Project Maintainers |\n| Requirement | The implementation shall not add any new external npm dependencies to package.json |\n| Rationale | Explicit requirement to maintain minimal dependency footprint |\n| Acceptance Criteria | After implementation, package.json devDependencies and dependencies remain unchanged (except @types/bun if already present) |\n\n### REQ-006: Display Success Confirmation\n\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Should Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | When validation succeeds, the system shall display a confirmation message |\n| Rationale | Provides positive feedback that validation completed and file is valid |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Given a valid YAML file, when validation succeeds, then a success message is displayed to stdout |\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Response Time | Validation of typical configuration files (under 1MB) should complete in under 1 second |\n| Memory Usage | Memory consumption should be proportional to file size |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| File Access | Only read access to specified file; no write operations |\n| Path Traversal | Accept only valid file paths within expected boundaries |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Error Clarity | Error messages should be understandable without consulting documentation |\n| Command Simplicity | Command invocation should follow standard CLI conventions |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNone identified for this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate Configuration File\n\n**As a** Developer\n**I want** to validate a YAML configuration file\n**So that** I can ensure it has no syntax errors before using it\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN a YAML file path\n- WHEN I run the yaml validator command with that path\n- THEN I receive feedback indicating whether the file is valid or invalid\n\n### STORY-002: Identify Syntax Errors\n\n**As a** Developer\n**I want** to see the location and nature of YAML syntax errors\n**So that** I can quickly fix problems in my configuration files\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error\n- WHEN I run the yaml validator command\n- THEN the error message includes the line number and description of the problem\n\n### STORY-003: Integrate Validation in CI\n\n**As a** CI/CD Pipeline\n**I want** the validator to return proper exit codes\n**So that** I can fail builds when configuration files are invalid\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN valid YAML, WHEN validated, THEN exit code is 0\n- GIVEN invalid YAML, WHEN validated, THEN exit code is non-zero\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| BR-001 | Empty files are considered invalid YAML |\n| BR-002 | Files that cannot be parsed as YAML are reported as invalid |\n| BR-003 | The validator operates on file content only; it does not validate against any schema |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| Constraint | Must use only Bun built-in capabilities or native JavaScript for YAML parsing |\n| Constraint | Must integrate with existing project structure (src/ directory) |\n| Dependency | Bun runtime environment |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Resolution | Source |\n|----------|------------|--------|\n| What YAML specification version? | YAML 1.1/1.2 (standard) | Conservative default |\n| Should batch validation be supported? | Out of scope for initial implementation | Inferred from \"simple\" requirement |\n| What about schema validation? | Out of scope; syntax validation only | Inferred from \"simple\" requirement |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None available | No PRD files found in .rp1/work/prds/ |\n| KB context | Best-effort inference | No index.md or concept_map.md found |\n| Project context | Bun TypeScript project | Inferred from package.json and tsconfig.json |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" - scope unclear | Single file validation only, no batch processing | Conservative interpretation of \"simple\" |\n| \"without adding new deps\" - which deps | No new entries in package.json dependencies or devDependencies | Explicit requirement interpretation |\n| User types not specified | Developer and CI/CD Pipeline | Standard CLI tool users |\n| Error handling behavior | Clear messages with exit codes | Standard CLI conventions |\n| YAML version support | Standard YAML 1.1/1.2 | Default assumption |\n| Empty file handling | Treated as invalid | Conservative default |\n"
