        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-01-30\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | Custom syntax validator | REQ-005 prohibits new npm dependencies; Bun has no built-in YAML parser | 1. Use js-yaml library (rejected - violates REQ-005), 2. Vendor external parser (rejected - complex, maintenance burden) |\n| D2 | Module organization | Separate yaml/ directory with types.ts and validator.ts | Clean separation of concerns, TypeScript best practices, allows future expansion | Single validator.ts file (rejected - mixing types and logic) |\n| D3 | CLI entry point location | src/validate-yaml.ts as separate entry | Follows existing pattern where index.ts is standalone entry; keeps concerns separated | Extend index.ts with subcommand routing (rejected - overcomplicates simple project) |\n| D4 | Error message format | Human-readable text with line numbers | REQ-002 requires clear error messages; developers need actionable feedback | JSON output only (rejected - harder for humans to read), Minimal messages (rejected - not actionable) |\n| D5 | Exit code strategy | 0 success, 1 for all failures | REQ-003 requires exit codes; simple convention works for CI/CD integration | Different codes per error type (rejected - adds complexity without clear benefit) |\n| D6 | Test file placement | Collocated in src/yaml/validator.test.ts | Follows existing project pattern of utils.test.ts next to utils.ts | Separate tests/ directory (rejected - inconsistent with existing patterns) |\n| D7 | File reading approach | Bun.file API | Built-in, no dependencies, performant, async-capable | Node fs module (rejected - Bun.file is more idiomatic for Bun projects) |\n| D8 | Empty file handling | Treat as invalid per BR-001 | Business rule explicitly states empty files are invalid YAML | Treat as valid empty document (rejected - violates BR-001) |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | Codebase analysis | Existing project uses Bun (package.json scripts, @types/bun) |\n| Language | TypeScript | Codebase analysis | All existing source files are .ts with strict mode |\n| Testing framework | bun:test | Codebase analysis | Existing tests use bun:test (src/utils.test.ts) |\n| Module system | ES Modules | Codebase analysis | package.json has \"type\": \"module\", existing code uses import/export |\n| File I/O | Bun.file API | Conservative default | Bun's built-in file API, no additional dependencies |\n| CLI argument parsing | process.argv | Codebase analysis | Existing index.ts uses process.argv[2] for arguments |\n| Output formatting | console.log/console.error | Codebase analysis | Standard output pattern used in existing code |\n\n## Risk Assessment\n\n| Risk | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|------|--------|------------|------------|\n| Custom YAML validator misses edge cases | Medium | Medium | Document limitations, focus on common syntax errors, comprehensive test coverage |\n| Performance issues with large files | Low | Low | Streaming read if needed, but typical YAML config files are small |\n| Inconsistent validation vs full YAML parsers | Medium | Medium | Clear documentation of scope as \"syntax validation\", not full parsing |\n\n## Assumptions Flagged for Validation\n\n| Assumption | Status | Notes |\n|------------|--------|-------|\n| A1: Bun provides YAML parsing capabilities | INVALID | Bun does NOT have built-in YAML parser; custom implementation required |\n| A2: Users provide file paths as arguments | Valid | Standard CLI convention |\n| A3: Files accessible on local filesystem | Valid | Standard assumption for CLI tools |\n| A4: YAML 1.1/1.2 specification | Partially addressed | Custom validator covers common syntax, not full specification |\n"
